WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.930
Joint sets divide rock into blocks and provide pathways along which

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.530
weathering can act. Their orientation can influence cliffs, pillars, notches, and

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.570
drainage. Recognize the fracture patterns in an outcrop before attributing them

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.270
to one particular stress history.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:21.170
Faults can produce aligned notches, scarps, crushed angular rock, displaced layers,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.990
and striations. Several observations are more persuasive than a straight valley

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.810
alone. A scarp shows relative topographic offset but does not by

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.290
itself identify the complete fault history.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:36.090
Resistant tilted beds can support ridges and dip slopes while weaker

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.910
units erode into lower ground.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.290
A ridge does not automatically mark an anticline, and a valley

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.230
does not automatically mark a syncline.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.410
Follow rock layers and ages through the landscape.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:51.850
Foliation, cleavage, and resistant folded layers can influence the shapes of

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.390
metamorphic outcrops. Modern topography is a product of both structure and

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.730
erosion. Distinguish a visible rock fabric from a hypothesis about when

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.270
it formed.
